{"id":4248,"date":"2024-06-17T12:41:43","date_gmt":"2024-06-17T11:41:43","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/?p=4248"},"modified":"2024-12-10T12:23:16","modified_gmt":"2024-12-10T12:23:16","slug":"children-display-radical-vision-for-london-flooded-by-climate-change-in-imagining-tomorrow-at-va-south-kensington","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/children-display-radical-vision-for-london-flooded-by-climate-change-in-imagining-tomorrow-at-va-south-kensington\/","title":{"rendered":"Children display radical vision for London flooded by climate change in \u2018Imagining Tomorrow\u2019 at V&amp;A South Kensington\u00a0 \u00a0"},"content":{"rendered":"\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Top designers and teachers transformed classrooms into labs and helped\u00a0children\u00a0to conceive and visualise their ideas for tackling flooding, extreme heat and cold.\u00a0The show highlights this creative collaboration.<\/strong><\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong><u>Not for publication before: 00.01 Monday 24 June 2024<\/u><\/strong><\/p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image aligncenter size-large is-resized\"><img decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"576\" data-src=\"https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/07\/Mangroves-1024x576.jpg\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-image-3536 lazyload\" style=\"--smush-placeholder-width: 1024px; --smush-placeholder-aspect-ratio: 1024\/576;width:967px;height:auto\" data-srcset=\"https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/07\/Mangroves-1024x576.jpg 1024w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/07\/Mangroves-300x169.jpg 300w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/07\/Mangroves-768x432.jpg 768w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/07\/Mangroves-1536x864.jpg 1536w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/07\/Mangroves-2048x1152.jpg 2048w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/07\/Mangroves-356x200.jpg 356w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/07\/Mangroves-200x113.jpg 200w\" data-sizes=\"(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml;base64,PHN2ZyB3aWR0aD0iMSIgaGVpZ2h0PSIxIiB4bWxucz0iaHR0cDovL3d3dy53My5vcmcvMjAwMC9zdmciPjwvc3ZnPg==\" \/><\/figure>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large is-resized\"><img decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"696\" data-src=\"https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/07\/Tyndale3-1024x696.jpg\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-image-3664 lazyload\" style=\"--smush-placeholder-width: 1024px; --smush-placeholder-aspect-ratio: 1024\/696;width:967px;height:auto\" data-srcset=\"https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/07\/Tyndale3-1024x696.jpg 1024w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/07\/Tyndale3-300x204.jpg 300w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/07\/Tyndale3-768x522.jpg 768w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/07\/Tyndale3-1536x1044.jpg 1536w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/07\/Tyndale3-2048x1392.jpg 2048w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/07\/Tyndale3-294x200.jpg 294w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/07\/Tyndale3-184x125.jpg 184w\" data-sizes=\"(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml;base64,PHN2ZyB3aWR0aD0iMSIgaGVpZ2h0PSIxIiB4bWxucz0iaHR0cDovL3d3dy53My5vcmcvMjAwMC9zdmciPjwvc3ZnPg==\" \/><\/figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><em>Flooded Highbury Fields conceived and visualised by the\u00a0children\u00a0of William Tyndale Primary School, London and interpreted by DaeWha Kang Design<\/em><\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">HOW should clothes, food, buildings, landscapes, and neighbourhoods change in the face of rising sea levels, freezing temperatures and extreme heat? Hundreds of primary school&nbsp;children&nbsp;across England have been tackling these questions alongside professional designers, who are helping turn&nbsp;children\u2019s classroom ideas into radical visions.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For the next six months, the V&amp;A South Kensington\u2019s Learning Centre is displaying some of the results of this unusual collaboration. Ideas on show in <strong><em>Imagining Tomorrow<\/em><\/strong>&nbsp;include a new urban landscape, conceived by young&nbsp;children, which represents a future London adapted to massive flooding that is anticipated in the capital.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The display features a selection of the ongoing work of the charity <strong>Climate Change All Change<\/strong>. It brings together top designers, educators, and environmentalists with primary schools. Together, they address a gap in primary education by integrating climate literacy and sustainable design into&nbsp;children\u2019s learning.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Through a mix of photographs and reproductions of 2D designs, the display includes innovative work by 9-10-year-olds, guided by teachers, practicing designers, and CCAC advisers. It outlines the stages of the CCAC\u2019s learning programme and culminates in the \u2018Designer\u2019s Reveal\u2019, featuring visual, digital interpretations of\u00a0children&#8217;s concepts designed to create a more sustainable world. (You can <a href=\"https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/schools-examples\/\">see examples here<\/a>.)<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">David Lloyd Jones, a leading green architect and CCAC co-founder, said:<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">\u201cPrimary schools are a crucible for creative thought and action. Yet, climate literacy is not included in the national curriculum. Meanwhile, the UK\u2019s reputation for design excellence is threatened because art and design teaching in schools has been eroded.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">\u201cCCAC aims to make climate and sustainable design education a standard component of the curriculum. We provide&nbsp;children&nbsp;with tools to understand the issues and means to envisage imaginative and effective accommodation of a turbulent and transformative climate future. CCAC\u2019s programme allows&nbsp;children, teachers, and designers to understand the global threat and see how it can be transcended.\u201d<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Dr Helen Charman, Director of Young V&amp;A, Learning and National Programmes at the V&amp;A commented:&nbsp;<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">\u201c<em>Imagining Tomorrow<\/em>&nbsp;is a timely reminder of how we must rethink the way we live. We know that climate change is on&nbsp;children\u2019s minds and this display will inspire&nbsp;children&nbsp;to think about design solutions that help address the impact of climate change, which they are already seeing in their daily lives.\u201d<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Educators are keenly supporting the initiative. A teacher at one of the participating schools,&nbsp;Ashton Gate Primary School, Bristol&nbsp;said:<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">\u201cThe Climate Change All Change programme showed us that the&nbsp;children&nbsp;can learn new and complex skills and use teamwork to present their ideas back to adults. It showed us how mature and capable the&nbsp;children&nbsp;can be when they are given the right level of challenge.\u201d<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong><em>Imagining Tomorrow<\/em><\/strong><strong>&nbsp;is at the John Lyon\u2019s Gallery in V&amp;A South Kensington\u2019s Learning Centre, until January 2025.&nbsp; Entry is free.<\/strong><\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Further information: <\/strong><strong><u><a href=\"https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/\">www.cc-ac.org<\/a><\/u><\/strong>&nbsp;or contact <strong>Linda Lloyd Jones <\/strong>at <strong><u><a href=\"mailto:linda@cc-ac.org\">linda@cc-ac.org<\/a><\/u><\/strong><strong>&nbsp; <\/strong>or on <strong>07500 108676.<\/strong><\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Additional and higher resolution images can be provided on request. \u00a0<\/strong><\/p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large is-resized\"><img decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"660\" data-src=\"https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2024\/06\/02_CCAC_Schools-1024x660.jpg\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-image-4250 lazyload\" style=\"--smush-placeholder-width: 1024px; --smush-placeholder-aspect-ratio: 1024\/660;width:1023px;height:auto\" data-srcset=\"https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2024\/06\/02_CCAC_Schools-1024x660.jpg 1024w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2024\/06\/02_CCAC_Schools-300x193.jpg 300w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2024\/06\/02_CCAC_Schools-768x495.jpg 768w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2024\/06\/02_CCAC_Schools-1536x990.jpg 1536w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2024\/06\/02_CCAC_Schools-2048x1320.jpg 2048w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2024\/06\/02_CCAC_Schools-310x200.jpg 310w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2024\/06\/02_CCAC_Schools-194x125.jpg 194w\" data-sizes=\"(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml;base64,PHN2ZyB3aWR0aD0iMSIgaGVpZ2h0PSIxIiB4bWxucz0iaHR0cDovL3d3dy53My5vcmcvMjAwMC9zdmciPjwvc3ZnPg==\" \/><\/figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><em>Imagining Tomorrow, displayed at the John Lyon\u2019s Gallery, V&amp;A South Kensington<\/em><\/p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"683\" data-src=\"https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/07\/Hassell-CCA-Coppermill-Primary-School-photo-by-Suzanne-Zhang-3-1024x683.jpg\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-image-3420 lazyload\" data-srcset=\"https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/07\/Hassell-CCA-Coppermill-Primary-School-photo-by-Suzanne-Zhang-3-1024x683.jpg 1024w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/07\/Hassell-CCA-Coppermill-Primary-School-photo-by-Suzanne-Zhang-3-300x200.jpg 300w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\